**Read:** [Gases and Applications — Linde](../sources/Gases-and-Applications-Linde.lingua#r_72580QNZ60FMQAARB8ZG7SRTFT), carbon dioxide and mixtures; shared industrial chemistry and chemical-supply quality material; complete primary process documentation for the selected source.

Recovered carbon dioxide carries the history of its source and conditioning. Fermentation, chemical processing and other sources can present different contaminants. Purification and phase handling must match the intended use. A high major-component fraction alone does not establish food, analytical or process suitability.

Follow water, volatile impurities, noncondensable gases and any source-specific substances through removal and rejected streams. Compression, cooling and phase changes alter storage and delivery behavior. Depressurization can change phase and restrict flow; treat the actual state path rather than assuming a permanent ideal gas.

**Work:** Compare two documented source routes at a common delivered specification. Trace an impurity through the process and identify its analytical evidence. Close a recovery balance that includes rejected product and handling losses. Explain how a change in the upstream source could invalidate an otherwise unchanged downstream operating description.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
